INSERT INTO `course_assessments` VALUES (1,'GRADE 7 - PRE-TEST - The School Repair Project','Pre-Test','Frustration',7,'<p>When the rainy season began, students at Mabini National High School noticed that several parts of their classroom needed repair. Some windows could no longer close properly, and rainwater entered through a small hole in the roof. The problem became especially difficult during afternoon classes.</p>\r\n<p>Their class adviser, Mr. Santos, asked the students to think of ways they could help. He explained that they could not perform dangerous repairs themselves, but they could help keep the classroom clean and organize materials for the repair workers.</p>\r\n<p>The students divided themselves into groups. One group listed the damaged parts of the room. Another group collected books and learning materials that needed to be moved away from the leaking area. A third group cleaned the classroom after the repair workers finished each day.</p>\r\n<p>The project took several weeks because some materials had to be ordered. Although the students became tired, they continued helping. They discovered that even simple tasks became easier when everyone worked together.</p>\r\n<p>When the repairs were finally completed, the classroom was safer and more comfortable. The students were proud of their work. They realized that taking responsibility did not always mean doing everything themselves. Sometimes, it meant knowing how to help safely and responsibly.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What problem did the students notice in their classroom?\",\"options\":[\"The classroom had too many chairs.\",\"Several parts of the classroom needed repair.\",\"The students did not have enough books.\",\"The classroom was too small.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why did rainwater enter the classroom?\",\"options\":[\"A window was broken.\",\"The door was missing.\",\"There was a hole in the roof.\",\"The floor was damaged.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did Mr. Santos ask the students to do?\",\"options\":[\"Repair the roof themselves.\",\"Leave the classroom permanently.\",\"Think of safe ways they could help.\",\"Ask another school for a classroom.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did the first group do?\",\"options\":[\"Cleaned the windows.\",\"Listed the damaged parts.\",\"Repaired the roof.\",\"Ordered construction materials.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why were some materials moved?\",\"options\":[\"They were going to be sold.\",\"They were old and useless.\",\"They needed protection from the leaking area.\",\"The students wanted new materials.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What made the project take several weeks?\",\"options\":[\"The students refused to help.\",\"The classroom was too large.\",\"Some materials had to be ordered.\",\"The teacher was absent.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did the students learn from the project?\",\"options\":[\"Working alone is always better.\",\"Responsibility includes helping safely.\",\"Repairs should always be done by students.\",\"Classroom projects are unnecessary.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which word best describes the students?\",\"options\":[\"Careless\",\"Uncooperative\",\"Responsible\",\"Impatient\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea of the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Students should repair school buildings.\",\"Teamwork can help students contribute responsibly to a school project.\",\"Teachers should do all classroom repairs.\",\"Rainy weather makes studying impossible.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred from the passage?\",\"options\":[\"The students learned that cooperation made the work easier.\",\"The students disliked their classroom.\",\"The repair workers refused to work.\",\"The school planned to close.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(2,'GRADE 7 - POST-TEST - The School Recycling Program','Post-Test','Frustration',7,'<p>Students at San Isidro National High School noticed that large amounts of paper and plastic were being thrown away every day. Their science teacher, Ms. Reyes, suggested creating a recycling program.</p>\r\n<p>The students first examined the school\'s waste bins. They discovered that many recyclable materials were mixed with food waste and other garbage. The students decided to place labeled containers in different areas of the school.</p>\r\n<p>Each class was assigned a responsibility. Some students collected recyclable materials, while others recorded the amount gathered each week. The science club also taught students how to separate paper, plastic, and other materials properly.</p>\r\n<p>During the first few weeks, the program did not work perfectly. Some students continued putting garbage into the wrong containers. Instead of giving up, the organizers placed clearer signs near the bins and reminded students about the purpose of the program.</p>\r\n<p>After several months, the amount of recyclable waste increased. The school also produced less mixed garbage. The students realized that successful environmental projects required patience, cooperation, and regular effort.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What problem did the students notice?\",\"options\":[\"Too many students were absent.\",\"Large amounts of recyclable material were being thrown away.\",\"The school had no science teacher.\",\"The classrooms had too many containers.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did the students discover when they examined the waste bins?\",\"options\":[\"They were completely empty.\",\"All waste was properly separated.\",\"Recyclable materials were mixed with other garbage.\",\"Students were not using the bins.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did the students place around the school?\",\"options\":[\"New classrooms\",\"Labeled recycling containers\",\"More desks\",\"Water tanks\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What did some students record?\",\"options\":[\"The number of teachers\",\"The amount of recyclable materials collected\",\"The number of classrooms\",\"The students\' test scores\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What problem occurred during the first few weeks?\",\"options\":[\"Students used the wrong containers.\",\"The recycling bins disappeared.\",\"The science club stopped meeting.\",\"There was no recyclable material.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How did the organizers respond to the problem?\",\"options\":[\"They cancelled the program.\",\"They removed all the containers.\",\"They added clearer signs and reminders.\",\"They punished every student.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What happened after several months?\",\"options\":[\"Recyclable waste increased and mixed garbage decreased.\",\"The recycling program was abandoned.\",\"Students stopped collecting materials.\",\"The school produced more garbage.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which quality helped the students continue the program?\",\"options\":[\"Laziness\",\"Patience\",\"Fear\",\"Competition\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"Recycling projects are difficult and useless.\",\"Environmental programs can succeed through cooperation and continuous effort.\",\"Students should avoid using plastic.\",\"Schools should remove all waste bins.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred from the passage?\",\"options\":[\"The students improved the program by responding to problems.\",\"The program succeeded immediately.\",\"The teacher did all the work.\",\"The students were not interested in recycling.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(3,'GRADE 8 - PRE-TEST - The Importance of Mangroves','Pre-Test','Frustration',8,'<p>Along many coastal communities in the Philippines, mangrove forests grow where land meets the sea. These trees are specially adapted to survive in salty water and muddy soil. Although they may look ordinary, mangroves provide important benefits to both people and wildlife.</p>\r\n<p>The tangled roots of mangrove trees help reduce the force of waves. During strong storms, these roots can help protect coastal areas from erosion. Mangrove forests also provide shelter for young fish, crabs, and other animals.</p>\r\n<p>Mangroves are also important to local communities. Fishermen may depend on healthy coastal ecosystems for their livelihood. When mangrove forests are damaged, the number of fish and other marine animals may decrease.</p>\r\n<p>Unfortunately, some mangrove areas have been removed to make space for buildings, roads, and other developments. Pollution can also harm these ecosystems.</p>\r\n<p>Many communities now participate in mangrove restoration projects. Volunteers plant young mangrove trees and protect existing forests. These efforts demonstrate that protecting nature can also protect people\'s livelihoods and communities.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Where do mangroves commonly grow?\",\"options\":[\"In deserts\",\"Where land meets the sea\",\"On mountain peaks\",\"In dry grasslands\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What helps mangroves survive in coastal areas?\",\"options\":[\"Their ability to live in salty water and muddy soil\",\"Their ability to grow without water\",\"Their ability to live only in forests\",\"Their ability to survive without sunlight\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How can mangrove roots help coastal communities?\",\"options\":[\"They increase the force of waves.\",\"They reduce wave force and erosion.\",\"They remove all seawater.\",\"They prevent rainfall.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What animals can find shelter in mangrove forests?\",\"options\":[\"Young fish and crabs\",\"Mountain goats\",\"Eagles only\",\"Farm animals\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why are mangroves important to fishermen?\",\"options\":[\"They provide buildings for fishermen.\",\"They support coastal ecosystems where fish can live.\",\"They make boats unnecessary.\",\"They prevent all storms.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one cause of mangrove destruction?\",\"options\":[\"Too much rainfall\",\"Building and development\",\"Too many fish\",\"Strong sunlight\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is mangrove restoration?\",\"options\":[\"Removing all mangrove trees\",\"Planting and protecting mangrove forests\",\"Building roads through forests\",\"Moving coastal communities\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the author\'s main purpose?\",\"options\":[\"To explain the importance of mangroves\",\"To teach readers how to build boats\",\"To describe different kinds of fish\",\"To explain how to become a fisherman\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which statement is supported by the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Mangroves are useful only to animals.\",\"Damaging mangroves can affect both nature and people.\",\"Mangroves cannot survive near the sea.\",\"Communities should remove mangroves.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred from the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Protecting mangroves can benefit both communities and ecosystems.\",\"Mangrove restoration has no effect on communities.\",\"Coastal communities do not depend on nature.\",\"Mangrove forests are dangerous to fish.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(4,'GRADE 8 - POST-TEST - Protecting Coral Reefs','Post-Test','Frustration',8,'<p>Coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems in the ocean. They provide shelter and food for many kinds of marine animals. Although coral reefs cover only a small part of the ocean floor, they support a large variety of life.</p>\r\n<p>Healthy coral reefs can also benefit coastal communities. They can help reduce the energy of waves before those waves reach shore. Reefs also support fishing and tourism, which provide income for many people.</p>\r\n<p>However, coral reefs face several threats. Pollution, destructive fishing practices, rising sea temperatures, and careless tourism can damage them. When corals become stressed, they may lose the tiny organisms that give them much of their color. This process is known as coral bleaching.</p>\r\n<p>Scientists and communities are working together to protect coral reefs. Some projects establish marine protected areas where fishing and other activities are limited. Other projects teach tourists how to visit reefs without damaging them.</p>\r\n<p>Protecting coral reefs requires cooperation. Governments, communities, tourists, and individuals all have a role in keeping marine ecosystems healthy.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What are coral reefs?\",\"options\":[\"Desert ecosystems\",\"Diverse marine ecosystems\",\"Freshwater lakes\",\"Mountain forests\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why are coral reefs important to marine animals?\",\"options\":[\"They provide food and shelter.\",\"They prevent animals from swimming.\",\"They remove seawater.\",\"They produce freshwater.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How can coral reefs benefit coastal communities?\",\"options\":[\"They eliminate storms completely.\",\"They support fishing and tourism.\",\"They prevent people from visiting beaches.\",\"They make boats unnecessary.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which is a threat to coral reefs?\",\"options\":[\"Responsible tourism\",\"Destructive fishing\",\"Marine protection\",\"Scientific research\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What happens during coral bleaching?\",\"options\":[\"Corals become healthier.\",\"Corals lose the organisms that give them color.\",\"Fish leave the ocean permanently.\",\"Coral reefs turn into forests.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one way communities protect reefs?\",\"options\":[\"Establishing marine protected areas\",\"Increasing destructive fishing\",\"Removing all marine animals\",\"Allowing unlimited pollution\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why do scientists teach tourists about responsible behavior?\",\"options\":[\"To encourage tourists to damage reefs\",\"To reduce harmful activities around reefs\",\"To stop all tourism\",\"To prevent people from swimming\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"Coral reefs are valuable ecosystems that require protection.\",\"Coral reefs are dangerous places.\",\"Tourism always damages reefs.\",\"Fishing should be completely stopped.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does the passage suggest about protecting coral reefs?\",\"options\":[\"Only scientists can protect them.\",\"Cooperation among different groups is necessary.\",\"Tourists have no responsibility.\",\"Governments cannot help.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred?\",\"options\":[\"Human activities can affect the health of marine ecosystems.\",\"Coral reefs are unaffected by pollution.\",\"Marine ecosystems do not benefit people.\",\"Coral bleaching always improves reefs.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(5,'GRADE 9 - PRE-TEST - Artificial Intelligence in the Classroom','Pre-Test','Frustration',9,'<p>Artificial intelligence, or AI, is becoming more common in schools. Some teachers use AI-powered tools to help create learning materials, provide explanations, or give students additional practice.</p>\r\n<p>One possible benefit of AI is that it can provide immediate feedback. For example, a student practicing grammar may receive information about mistakes without waiting for the next class meeting. AI tools can also present information in different ways depending on the learner\'s needs.</p>\r\n<p>However, AI should not replace teachers. A computer program may provide an answer, but it cannot completely understand a student\'s emotions, personal experiences, or difficulties. Teachers also help students develop communication, judgment, and social skills.</p>\r\n<p>There are also concerns about accuracy and responsible use. AI systems can sometimes provide incorrect information. Students who depend too heavily on AI may also fail to develop their own problem-solving skills.</p>\r\n<p>For these reasons, schools should teach students how to use AI responsibly. AI can be a useful educational tool when it supports learning rather than replacing the effort and thinking required from students.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does AI stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Automatic Information\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Advanced Internet\",\"Applied Instruction\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How can AI provide immediate feedback?\",\"options\":[\"By giving information about mistakes quickly\",\"By replacing teachers\",\"By removing assignments\",\"By preventing students from practicing\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one possible benefit of AI?\",\"options\":[\"It can provide additional learning practice.\",\"It eliminates the need for teachers.\",\"It guarantees correct information.\",\"It prevents students from making mistakes.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why should AI not completely replace teachers?\",\"options\":[\"Teachers can understand students in ways AI cannot.\",\"Teachers cannot use technology.\",\"AI cannot provide information.\",\"Teachers do not need technology.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one concern about AI?\",\"options\":[\"It can sometimes provide incorrect information.\",\"It always gives correct answers.\",\"It cannot process information.\",\"It cannot be used in education.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What may happen if students depend too heavily on AI?\",\"options\":[\"Their problem-solving skills may not develop properly.\",\"They will automatically become better readers.\",\"They will no longer need education.\",\"They will understand every subject perfectly.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does the author recommend?\",\"options\":[\"Schools should completely ban technology.\",\"Students should use AI responsibly.\",\"Teachers should stop teaching.\",\"Students should let AI do all their work.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"AI can support education but should be used responsibly.\",\"AI should replace every teacher.\",\"Students should never use technology.\",\"AI is always harmful.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does \\\"depend too heavily\\\" most nearly mean?\",\"options\":[\"Use something more than is appropriate\",\"Avoid something completely\",\"Study something carefully\",\"Understand something quickly\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred from the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Good use of AI requires students to continue thinking for themselves.\",\"AI can solve every educational problem.\",\"Teachers are no longer necessary.\",\"Students should never use AI tools.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(6,'GRADE 9 - POST-TEST - Technology and Independent Learning','Post-Test','Frustration',9,'<p>Digital technology has changed the way students find and use information. Instead of depending only on printed books, students can access digital libraries, educational videos, online articles, and interactive learning platforms.</p>\r\n<p>These resources can make learning more flexible. A student who does not understand a lesson can review a video or read another explanation. Students can also explore topics that interest them beyond what is discussed in class.</p>\r\n<p>However, having access to information does not automatically mean that students are learning effectively. The internet contains information that may be inaccurate, outdated, or misleading. Students therefore need to evaluate sources before accepting information as true.</p>\r\n<p>Independent learning also requires discipline. Without a teacher constantly monitoring them, students may become distracted by games, social media, or unrelated websites. Setting goals and managing time can help students use technology productively.</p>\r\n<p>Technology is therefore most useful when combined with critical thinking and self-discipline. Digital tools can open many opportunities for learning, but students must still take responsibility for how they use them.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How has technology changed learning?\",\"options\":[\"It has made information less available.\",\"It has given students access to many digital resources.\",\"It has eliminated the need for books.\",\"It has prevented students from studying independently.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can students do if they do not understand a lesson?\",\"options\":[\"Stop studying\",\"Review another explanation or learning resource\",\"Avoid asking questions\",\"Delete their notes\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why should students evaluate online sources?\",\"options\":[\"Some information online may be inaccurate or misleading.\",\"All online information is correct.\",\"Online information is never useful.\",\"Teachers do not allow online resources.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one challenge of independent learning?\",\"options\":[\"Students may become distracted.\",\"Students cannot access information.\",\"Students have too many teachers.\",\"Students cannot set goals.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can help students use technology productively?\",\"options\":[\"Avoiding all digital tools\",\"Setting goals and managing time\",\"Spending more time on social media\",\"Ignoring unreliable information\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does the passage say about digital resources?\",\"options\":[\"They can provide additional learning opportunities.\",\"They are always accurate.\",\"They should replace teachers.\",\"They are useful only for entertainment.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What skill is important when using online information?\",\"options\":[\"Critical thinking\",\"Memorization only\",\"Speed reading only\",\"Drawing\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"Technology makes teachers unnecessary.\",\"Digital technology can support independent learning when used responsibly.\",\"Students should avoid the internet.\",\"Online information is always reliable.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does \\\"misleading\\\" most nearly mean?\",\"options\":[\"Likely to give a wrong impression\",\"Easy to understand\",\"Completely accurate\",\"Difficult to access\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred?\",\"options\":[\"Students remain responsible for their own learning even when technology is available.\",\"Technology guarantees academic success.\",\"Students do not need critical thinking when using the internet.\",\"Independent learning requires no discipline.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(7,'GRADE 10 - PRE-TEST - Protecting Historical Places','Pre-Test','Frustration',10,'<p>Historical places provide communities with connections to the past. Old buildings, archaeological sites, monuments, and other cultural locations can help people understand how earlier generations lived.</p>\r\n<p>In the Philippines, many historical places are found in different regions. Some contain evidence of ancient communities, while others are connected to important events in more recent history. These places can also attract visitors and contribute to local tourism.</p>\r\n<p>However, historical sites can be damaged by natural disasters, pollution, construction, vandalism, and neglect. Once an important structure or artifact is destroyed, some information about the past may be impossible to recover.</p>\r\n<p>Preserving historical places requires cooperation. Government agencies can establish rules and provide funding, while local communities can help protect sites and educate visitors. Schools can also encourage young people to learn about their local history.</p>\r\n<p>Protecting cultural heritage is not simply about preserving old objects. It is about maintaining knowledge and identity for future generations. When communities value their history, they are more likely to protect the places that tell their stories.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why are historical places important?\",\"options\":[\"They provide connections to the past.\",\"They are always modern buildings.\",\"They prevent natural disasters.\",\"They eliminate tourism.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can archaeological sites contain?\",\"options\":[\"Evidence of ancient communities\",\"Modern technology only\",\"New buildings\",\"Artificial materials only\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How can historical places benefit communities?\",\"options\":[\"They can contribute to tourism.\",\"They prevent all construction.\",\"They eliminate local businesses.\",\"They stop people from learning.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which can damage historical sites?\",\"options\":[\"Proper maintenance\",\"Education\",\"Vandalism\",\"Responsible tourism\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why is the destruction of an artifact serious?\",\"options\":[\"It may cause information about the past to be lost.\",\"It always improves a historical site.\",\"It makes history easier to study.\",\"It increases the number of artifacts.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Who can help preserve historical places?\",\"options\":[\"Only tourists\",\"Only students\",\"Government agencies, communities, and schools\",\"Only business owners\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does cultural heritage include?\",\"options\":[\"Knowledge and places connected to a community\'s past\",\"Only modern buildings\",\"Only tourist attractions\",\"Only natural resources\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"Historical places should be protected because they preserve cultural knowledge and identity.\",\"Historical places are useful only for tourism.\",\"Communities should replace old buildings with new ones.\",\"Students should avoid learning history.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does \\\"preserving\\\" most nearly mean?\",\"options\":[\"Protecting something so it continues to exist\",\"Destroying something\",\"Moving something without care\",\"Ignoring something\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred?\",\"options\":[\"Young people can play a role in protecting cultural heritage.\",\"Historical preservation is only the government\'s responsibility.\",\"Historical sites have no educational value.\",\"Tourism always destroys historical places.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(8,'GRADE 10 - POST-TEST - Learning from the Past','Post-Test','Frustration',10,'<p>History is more than a collection of dates and names. It is a record of people\'s experiences, decisions, achievements, and mistakes. Studying history can help communities understand how the present was shaped.</p>\r\n<p>Museums, historical documents, artifacts, and archaeological discoveries are some of the sources used to study the past. Each source provides information, but researchers must examine evidence carefully. A single object or document may not tell the entire story.</p>\r\n<p>Archaeology is particularly useful because it provides physical evidence about earlier societies. Tools, pottery, buildings, and other remains can reveal information about how people lived, worked, traded, and interacted with their environment.</p>\r\n<p>Modern technology has also changed historical research. Digital databases, three-dimensional imaging, and other technologies allow researchers to examine and preserve information more efficiently. Some discoveries can now be studied by people in different parts of the world.</p>\r\n<p>Despite these advances, preserving historical evidence remains important. Future researchers may ask questions that people today cannot yet imagine. Protecting evidence from the past gives future generations an opportunity to make new discoveries and develop a deeper understanding of human history.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does history record?\",\"options\":[\"Only dates and names\",\"People\'s experiences, decisions, achievements, and mistakes\",\"Only archaeological objects\",\"Only modern events\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which is a source of historical information?\",\"options\":[\"Artifacts\",\"Advertisements only\",\"Weather forecasts\",\"Modern entertainment\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why must researchers examine evidence carefully?\",\"options\":[\"One source may not tell the entire story.\",\"Historical sources are always incorrect.\",\"Researchers do not need evidence.\",\"Artifacts have no value.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can archaeology reveal?\",\"options\":[\"How earlier societies lived and worked\",\"Only future events\",\"Modern social media trends\",\"Weather conditions\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How has technology helped historical research?\",\"options\":[\"It makes evidence disappear.\",\"It allows researchers to examine and preserve information more efficiently.\",\"It prevents researchers from sharing information.\",\"It eliminates the need for evidence.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is one example of modern technology mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Three-dimensional imaging\",\"Television broadcasting\",\"Online gaming\",\"Digital music\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why is preserving historical evidence important?\",\"options\":[\"Future researchers may discover new information from it.\",\"Historical evidence has no future value.\",\"Researchers should avoid studying the past.\",\"Old objects should always be replaced.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"options\":[\"Studying and preserving historical evidence helps people understand the past and make future discoveries.\",\"Technology has made history unnecessary.\",\"Archaeology is the only way to study history.\",\"Historical documents should not be preserved.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does \\\"evidence\\\" mean in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Information or material used to support knowledge about something\",\"A personal opinion without support\",\"A fictional story\",\"A future prediction\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred from the passage?\",\"options\":[\"New technology can improve historical research, but preserving original evidence remains important.\",\"Technology has completely replaced historical evidence.\",\"Future researchers will have no interest in history.\",\"Archaeological discoveries are no longer useful.\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-08 15:16:52','2026-10-11 10:42:05'),(9,'GRADE 7 - PRE-TEST - The Power of Renewable Energy','Pre-Test','Instructional',7,'<p>When strong typhoons struck the island of Isla Verde, the community often suffered from weeks of power outages. The local fishing cooperative relied on costly, noisy diesel generators that emitted thick black smoke and frequently ran out of fuel during prolonged storms. Without electricity, children struggled to study at night, and fish catches could not be stored in freezers.</p><p>To solve this ongoing crisis, a youth council partnered with clean energy engineers to install a community solar micro-grid. They mounted durable solar panels on the roof of the multi-purpose civic hall and connected them to rechargeable lithium batteries. In addition, small solar-powered street lamps were placed along the shoreline where fishermen docked their wooden boats.</p><p>The transformation was immediate. Families saved money previously spent on expensive diesel and kerosene. The cooperative established a communal cold storage unit powered entirely by sunlight, allowing fishermen to preserve their catch and sell at fair market prices. Most importantly, students gathered at the lighted community hall each evening to complete their school assignments safely.</p><p>The success of the Isla Verde project proved that remote rural communities do not need to wait for large commercial power plants to enjoy reliable electricity. Through collective action and sustainable technology, small islands can build clean and resilient power systems on their own.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What caused frequent power outages on the island of Isla Verde?\",\"options\":[\"Strong seasonal typhoons\",\"Earthquakes and landslides\",\"Volcanic eruptions\",\"Severe prolonged droughts\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What source of power did the fishing cooperative rely on before the project?\",\"options\":[\"A geothermal power plant\",\"Costly and noisy diesel generators\",\"Hydroelectric water wheels\",\"A commercial coal plant\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What was a major disadvantage of using the diesel generators?\",\"options\":[\"They were too quiet to notice\",\"They emitted thick smoke and often ran out of fuel\",\"They required sunlight to run\",\"They were completely free to operate\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Where were the new solar panels mounted?\",\"options\":[\"On individual fishing boats\",\"On the roof of the multi-purpose civic hall\",\"On floating rafts out at sea\",\"Along the sandy beach shoreline\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How did the solar micro-grid help the fishermen preserve their livelihood?\",\"options\":[\"It powered a communal cold storage unit for fish catches\",\"It built larger motorized fishing boats\",\"It provided free diesel fuel for boat engines\",\"It cleaned the saltwater in the bay\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Where did students gather each evening to study safely?\",\"options\":[\"At the lighted community hall\",\"Inside dark private homes\",\"On the docking pier\",\"At the local market stalls\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What financial benefit did island families experience after the solar installation?\",\"options\":[\"They received cash payments from the city\",\"They saved money previously spent on diesel and kerosene\",\"They stopped buying food and supplies\",\"They sold electricity to neighboring islands\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which word best describes the Isla Verde clean energy initiative?\",\"options\":[\"Wasteful\",\"Short-lived\",\"Sustainable\",\"Impractical\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the author\'s main purpose in writing this passage?\",\"options\":[\"To describe how to repair a diesel generator\",\"To illustrate how community cooperation and clean energy can solve local crises\",\"To discourage people from living on remote islands\",\"To explain the scientific physics of solar cells\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What conclusion can be drawn from the success of the Isla Verde project?\",\"options\":[\"Small communities can build clean and resilient power systems through collective action\",\"Islands must always rely on mainland power grids\",\"Diesel fuel is more practical than solar energy\",\"Solar power works only during clear summer months\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(10,'GRADE 7 - POST-TEST - Protecting Mangrove Ecosystems','Post-Test','Instructional',7,'<p>Along the coastal shores of Oriental Mindoro, thick mangrove forests once formed a green shield between the stormy sea and coastal villages. Over the past decades, large areas of these vital wetlands were cleared for fishpond development and firewood gathering. When severe tropical storms swept across the coast, villages without mangrove buffers experienced violent wave surges, severe shoreline erosion, and heavy flooding.</p><p>Recognizing the danger, local high school students and community elders launched a coastal rehabilitation project called \'Bakawan Buhay.\' Every weekend, volunteers gathered propagules, the slender seedling pods dropped by mature mangrove trees, and nurtured them in temporary nurseries. Once the seedlings developed sturdy root systems, teams waded into the muddy intertidal mudflats to plant them in alternating rows.</p><p>Mangrove trees possess intricate, tangled root systems that perform several essential ecological functions. Above the water, their roots dissipate strong wave energy, reducing surge height before water reaches populated settlements. Beneath the water, the sheltered root mazes provide vital feeding and breeding grounds for juvenile fish, crabs, and shrimp.</p><p>Within three years, the newly planted mangroves matured into a dense coastal wetland. Local fishermen reported higher daily catches of crabs and fish, while storm surges during the monsoon season caused minimal shoreline damage. The community learned that protecting natural coastal defenses is far more effective and affordable than building concrete seawalls.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why were the coastal mangrove forests cleared in previous decades?\",\"options\":[\"For fishpond development and firewood gathering\",\"To make room for deep-water ports\",\"To construct coastal tourist resorts\",\"To build large concrete airports\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What happened to coastal villages after the mangrove trees were removed?\",\"options\":[\"They experienced violent wave surges and severe erosion\",\"Their fish catches increased dramatically\",\"They became cooler and less humid\",\"Rainfall stopped completely along the coast\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What was the official name of the community rehabilitation project?\",\"options\":[\"Luntiang Baybayin\",\"Bakawan Buhay\",\"Sagip Karagatan\",\"Bantay Dagat\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What are \'propagules\' as described in the reading passage?\",\"options\":[\"Heavy wooden stakes used to anchor boats\",\"Slender seedling pods dropped by mature mangrove trees\",\"Chemical fertilizers used in commercial fishponds\",\"Artificial concrete blocks used as wave barriers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How do mangrove roots protect coastal villages above the water line?\",\"options\":[\"They catch rainwater for drinking\",\"They dissipate strong wave energy and reduce surge height\",\"They block wind from reaching houses\",\"They reflect sunlight back into the atmosphere\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What ecological role do underwater mangrove roots provide for marine wildlife?\",\"options\":[\"They trap predatory sharks\",\"They serve as feeding and breeding grounds for young fish, crabs, and shrimp\",\"They absorb saltwater to create freshwater streams\",\"They eliminate marine bacteria\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How long did it take for the planted seedlings to mature into a dense wetland?\",\"options\":[\"Six months\",\"One full year\",\"Three years\",\"Ten years\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What economic improvement did local fishermen report after the mangroves matured?\",\"options\":[\"Higher daily catches of crabs and fish\",\"Cheaper boat engine repairs\",\"Higher fuel prices\",\"Lower cost of fishing nets\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What major lesson did the community learn from the Bakawan Buhay project?\",\"options\":[\"Concrete seawalls are always superior to trees\",\"Natural coastal defenses are more effective and affordable than artificial seawalls\",\"Mangroves should be harvested regularly for firewood\",\"Coastal restoration requires expensive foreign machinery\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the main idea of the entire passage?\",\"options\":[\"Mangrove timber makes high-quality charcoal\",\"Typhoons cannot be prevented in the Philippines\",\"Community-led mangrove restoration protects coastlines and restores marine biodiversity\",\"Commercial fishponds are necessary for national food supply\"],\"correct\":2}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(11,'GRADE 8 - PRE-TEST - Indigenous Engineering: The Rice Terraces','Pre-Test','Instructional',8,'<p>Carved into the steep mountain slopes of Ifugao over two thousand years ago, the Rice Terraces stand as a monumental achievement in indigenous engineering. Built entirely by hand using primitive stone and wooden tools, these stepping-stone agricultural fields rise thousands of feet into the Cordillera mountain range. Ancient builders moved millions of tons of earth and rock without modern machinery to create productive farmland on terrain once thought impossible to cultivate.</p><p>The genius of the rice terraces lies in their sophisticated hydraulic design. Water originates from high-altitude rainforests, which act as natural sponges absorbing heavy tropical rainfall. Ancient engineers constructed a network of hand-carved stone canals, bamboo conduits, and sluice gates that channeled mountain runoff evenly from the highest tier down to the lowest terrace. This gravity-fed system ensures that every parcel of land receives adequate water without causing catastrophic landslides or soil erosion.</p><p>Furthermore, terrace maintenance relies on an indigenous system of cooperative labor known as \'ubbu.\' When retaining walls collapse after torrential typhoons, farming clans pool their labor to gather river stones and rebuild damaged terraces without exchanging money. Traditional pest management and water rotation schedules are regulated by village elders in accordance with seasonal astronomical observations.</p><p>Today, the terraces face serious challenges, including climate-induced droughts and younger generations migrating to urban commercial centers. Preserving this living cultural landscape requires not only physical repair but also honoring the deep indigenous knowledge systems that harmonize food production with ecological preservation.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Where are the ancient Rice Terraces located?\",\"options\":[\"On the plains of Central Luzon\",\"In the steep mountain slopes of Ifugao\",\"Along the coastal hills of Palawan\",\"In the volcanic valleys of Albay\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What tools were used by ancient builders to construct the terraces?\",\"options\":[\"Modern bulldozers and excavators\",\"Primitive stone and wooden tools\",\"Steam-powered machinery\",\"Heavy iron drilling equipment\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Where does the water for the terrace irrigation system originate?\",\"options\":[\"Underground thermal springs\",\"High-altitude rainforests\",\"Man-made concrete reservoirs\",\"Coastal desalination facilities\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How does the gravity-fed canal network prevent landslides?\",\"options\":[\"By pumping water back up into the clouds\",\"By channeling mountain runoff evenly from high to low tiers\",\"By drying out the soil completely\",\"By diverting all rainfall into deep caves\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the traditional Ifugao cooperative labor system called?\",\"options\":[\"Bayanihan\",\"Ubbu\",\"Pagtutulungan\",\"Kasama\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How do farming clans rebuild damaged terrace walls after severe typhoons?\",\"options\":[\"They hire commercial construction companies\",\"They pool collective labor to gather river stones without exchanging money\",\"They abandon damaged fields permanently\",\"They replace stone walls with concrete barriers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How are water rotation schedules and pest management regulated?\",\"options\":[\"By government agricultural inspectors\",\"By village elders using seasonal astronomical observations\",\"By computer automated weather systems\",\"By commercial pesticide dealers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What modern challenges threaten the continued survival of the terraces?\",\"options\":[\"Overabundance of farm workers and flooding\",\"Climate-induced droughts and youth migration to urban centers\",\"Excessive foreign tourism and lack of rain\",\"Industrial mining inside the terraces\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does the term \'hydraulic design\' mean in the context of the passage?\",\"options\":[\"A method for extracting precious metals\",\"An engineering system designed to manage and channel water flow\",\"A technique for carving wooden farm implements\",\"A mathematical formula for measuring slope height\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the author\'s main conclusion regarding the preservation of the terraces?\",\"options\":[\"The terraces should be converted into commercial tourist resorts\",\"Preservation requires honoring indigenous knowledge systems alongside physical repairs\",\"Modern machinery should replace all manual farming traditions\",\"The terraces have outlived their agricultural usefulness\"],\"correct\":1}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(12,'GRADE 8 - POST-TEST - Coral Reef Conservation in the Coral Triangle','Post-Test','Instructional',8,'<p>The marine waters of the Philippines lie at the very heart of the Coral Triangle, a vast tropical oceanic region often referred to as the \'Amazon of the Seas.\' Despite occupying less than one percent of the global ocean surface, this biodiverse region contains over seventy-five percent of all known coral species and supports thousands of marine creatures, ranging from microscopic plankton to giant manta rays and endangered sea turtles.</p><p>Corals are not inanimate minerals; they are colonial marine invertebrates composed of thousands of tiny organisms called polyps. These polyps live in an intricate symbiotic relationship with microscopic photosynthetic algae known as zooxanthellae. The algae reside inside the translucent tissues of the coral, producing essential nutrients through photosynthesis and giving corals their vibrant coloration. In exchange, coral polyps supply the algae with shelter and vital carbon dioxide.</p><p>This fragile relationship is increasingly endangered by rising sea temperatures and marine pollution. When sea temperatures rise even one or two degrees above average for extended periods, heat-stressed corals expel their symbiotic algae in a distressing process known as coral bleaching. Deprived of their primary food producers, the ghostly white coral skeletons weaken and will perish unless water conditions stabilize quickly.</p><p>To combat this crisis, coastal municipalities across the archipelago are establishing community-enforced Marine Protected Areas (MPAs). In these designated sanctuaries, commercial fishing and anchoring are strictly prohibited, giving degraded reefs a protected window to regenerate. Studies show that well-managed MPAs not only revive coral health but also create a \'spillover effect,\' boosting fish populations in surrounding open waters.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why is the Coral Triangle referred to as the \'Amazon of the Seas\'?\",\"options\":[\"It has dense underwater rainforest trees\",\"It possesses extraordinary marine biodiversity and most known coral species\",\"It is located along the Amazon River in South America\",\"It is the coldest oceanic region on the planet\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What are corals scientifically classified as in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Non-living sedimentary rock formations\",\"Colonial marine invertebrates composed of tiny polyps\",\"A species of large marine flowering plants\",\"Deep-sea freshwater minerals\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What are zooxanthellae, and what is their role in coral biology?\",\"options\":[\"Parasitic bacteria that destroy coral structures\",\"Photosynthetic algae that produce nutrients and vibrant color\",\"Tiny crustaceans that bore holes into limestone\",\"Predatory fish that feed on coral polyps\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What do coral polyps provide to the symbiotic algae in return?\",\"options\":[\"Shelter and vital carbon dioxide\",\"Direct sunlight and cold water\",\"Calcium carbonate food tablets\",\"Protection from deep ocean currents\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What causes the destructive process known as \'coral bleaching\'?\",\"options\":[\"Overpopulation of manta rays and sea turtles\",\"Heat stress from rising ocean temperatures causing corals to expel algae\",\"Lack of salt in oceanic waters\",\"Excessive depth of the coral beds\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What happens to corals if bleaching conditions persist for an extended period?\",\"options\":[\"They grow twice as fast as before\",\"They transform into hard granite rock\",\"They weaken and perish from lack of nutrition\",\"They migrate to colder arctic waters\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What conservation measure are coastal municipalities enforcing across the country?\",\"options\":[\"Commercial dredging of the sea floor\",\"Establishing community-enforced Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)\",\"Constructing artificial islands out of plastic waste\",\"Introducing predatory fish into coral lagoons\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which human activities are strictly prohibited inside designated marine sanctuaries?\",\"options\":[\"Scientific observation and snorkeling\",\"Commercial fishing and boat anchoring\",\"Swimming and photography\",\"Rainwater collection\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the \'spillover effect\' described in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Oil leaks spreading from damaged tanker ships\",\"Marine protected areas boosting fish populations in nearby open fishing zones\",\"Coral polyps falling onto sandy sea floors\",\"Storm surges flooding coastal settlements\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What can be inferred about the future of coral reefs from this passage?\",\"options\":[\"Their survival depends on reducing global warming and maintaining local protected areas\",\"Corals are indestructible and do not need human protection\",\"Marine sanctuaries do not benefit coastal fishermen\",\"Reefs can survive without photosynthetic algae indefinitely\"],\"correct\":0}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(13,'GRADE 9 - PRE-TEST - Critical Thinking in the Digital Media Age','Pre-Test','Instructional',9,'<p>In today\'s interconnected digital landscape, information circulates faster than at any time in human history. Social media algorithms are deliberately engineered to maximize user engagement by promoting sensational, emotionally charged, and polarizing content. As a consequence, digital consumers frequently find themselves enclosed within \'filter bubbles\' and echo chambers that reinforce existing beliefs while systematically hiding alternative perspectives.</p><p>This automated environment has accelerated the rapid spread of both misinformation, which is false information shared without harmful intent, and disinformation, which is deliberately fabricated falsehood designed to deceive or manipulate the public. Recent advancements in artificial intelligence have also introduced synthetic media, commonly known as \'deepfakes,\' making it increasingly difficult to discern genuine audiovisual recordings from fabricated ones using visual inspection alone.</p><p>To navigate this complex information ecosystem responsibly, educators emphasize the practice of \'lateral reading.\' Unlike traditional vertical reading, where a reader remains on a single website to evaluate its appearance and claims, lateral reading requires users to leave the initial web page immediately. Fact-checkers open multiple browser tabs to investigate the organization behind the publication, verify author credentials, cross-reference assertions against established primary sources, and evaluate funding transparency.</p><p>Ultimately, becoming an informed digital citizen requires intellectual humility and disciplined verification habits. Rather than passively accepting captivating headlines or confirming preconceived biases, critical thinkers pause to verify before sharing. In an age of algorithmic amplification, evaluating information integrity is no longer merely an academic skill; it is a vital civic responsibility.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the primary engineering objective of social media algorithms as explained in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To educate users on scientific topics\",\"To maximize engagement by promoting sensational and emotionally charged content\",\"To verify the accuracy of news articles before posting\",\"To connect users with opposing points of view\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is an \'echo chamber\' in the context of digital media consumption?\",\"options\":[\"An online audio recording studio\",\"An information bubble that reinforces existing views while filtering out opposing views\",\"A website with superior sound quality\",\"A chat room used exclusively by academic researchers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How does the passage distinguish misinformation from disinformation?\",\"options\":[\"Misinformation is shared without malicious intent, while disinformation is deliberately deceptive\",\"Misinformation is printed on paper, while disinformation is digital\",\"Misinformation is always illegal, while disinformation is harmless\",\"There is no difference between the two terms\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is \'synthetic media\' commonly referred to in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Viral memes\",\"Deepfakes\",\"Podcasts\",\"Infographics\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why is simple visual inspection no longer adequate to verify audio and video recordings?\",\"options\":[\"Screens have lower resolution than before\",\"AI technologies can generate hyper-realistic fabricated media\",\"Digital videos are too short to analyze\",\"Modern cameras have distorted lenses\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the core method of \'lateral reading\'?\",\"options\":[\"Reading from right to left across a printed page\",\"Opening multiple browser tabs to verify the source through external corroboration\",\"Reading only the comments section of an article\",\"Staying on a single website and admiring its graphic design\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How does lateral reading differ from vertical reading?\",\"options\":[\"Vertical reading investigates external sources; lateral reading reads vertically\",\"Vertical reading stays on one website; lateral reading checks external credibility\",\"Vertical reading is done by fact-checkers; lateral reading is done by algorithms\",\"Vertical reading is faster than lateral reading\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which factor should fact-checkers examine when evaluating source credibility?\",\"options\":[\"Author credentials, primary sources, and funding transparency\",\"The number of likes and shares on social media\",\"The bright colors of the website banner\",\"The speed at which the website loads images\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What essential habit does the author recommend for critical digital thinkers?\",\"options\":[\"Sharing breaking news instantly before others\",\"Pausing to verify information integrity before reacting or sharing\",\"Relying exclusively on content recommended by social algorithms\",\"Avoiding the internet entirely\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Why does the author consider information evaluation a \'vital civic responsibility\'?\",\"options\":[\"It helps social media companies earn advertising profits\",\"Unchecked falsehoods can deceive citizens and undermine democratic decision-making\",\"It guarantees good grades on standardized examinations\",\"It reduces electricity consumption on mobile phones\"],\"correct\":1}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(14,'GRADE 9 - POST-TEST - Telemedicine in Island Communities','Post-Test','Instructional',9,'<p>Providing equitable healthcare across the Philippine archipelago has long been hindered by extreme geographic fragmentation. Remote island municipalities, categorized as Geographically Isolated and Disadvantaged Areas (GIDA), often lack resident medical specialists, diagnostic laboratory equipment, and surgical operating facilities. For sick villagers, obtaining advanced medical attention previously required expensive, multi-hour boat journeys across hazardous seas to mainland provincial hospitals.</p><p>To overcome these persistent barriers, the Department of Health partnered with university researchers to implement telemedicine networks in remote coastal outposts. Rural health units were equipped with high-speed satellite internet receivers, digital stethoscopes, portable ultrasound devices, and cloud-synchronized electronic health record systems. Local community nurses and midwives were trained to perform preliminary clinical examinations using these digital tools.</p><p>Through encrypted video conferencing, provincial physicians and specialist doctors in metropolitan hospitals can now evaluate patients in real time. A dermatologist hundreds of kilometers away can inspect skin lesions via high-resolution video, while a cardiologist in Manila can review real-time electrocardiogram (ECG) data transmitted directly from an island clinic. Prescriptions and individualized therapy plans are issued immediately, allowing patients to receive targeted treatment without leaving their hometowns.</p><p>While telemedicine cannot replace emergency surgical facilities, it has dramatically reduced unnecessary medical transfers, prevented disease complications through early detection, and lessened financial distress for rural families. Expanding rural electrical grids and ensuring reliable satellite bandwidth will be vital to sustaining this digital health revolution across all island frontiers.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What does the acronym \'GIDA\' represent in Philippine healthcare planning?\",\"options\":[\"General Island Diagnostic Authority\",\"Geographically Isolated and Disadvantaged Areas\",\"Government Institute for Digital Assistance\",\"Global Immunization Development Agency\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What major hardship did island villagers face when seeking specialist medical care in the past?\",\"options\":[\"Navigating complex online scheduling systems\",\"Costly and hazardous boat journeys to mainland hospitals\",\"Excessive numbers of competing hospitals on the island\",\"Having too many doctors visit their homes\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What technological infrastructure was supplied to the rural health clinics?\",\"options\":[\"Commercial advertising screens\",\"Satellite internet, digital diagnostic devices, and electronic health records\",\"Heavy diesel mining generators\",\"Automated pharmacy vending kiosks\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Who conducts the initial patient examinations at the rural island health units?\",\"options\":[\"Visiting mainland tourists\",\"Local community nurses and midwives trained in digital tools\",\"School teachers and volunteers\",\"Military medical officers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How do specialist doctors in metropolitan hospitals examine remote patients?\",\"options\":[\"Through encrypted real-time video conferences and synchronized digital data\",\"By sending handwritten letters through the postal mail\",\"By asking patients to travel to Manila each week\",\"Through automated telephone answering machines\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What specific medical specialty example is mentioned regarding cardiac care?\",\"options\":[\"A brain surgeon performing surgery via satellite\",\"A cardiologist in Manila reviewing real-time electrocardiogram (ECG) data\",\"An optometrist prescribing eyeglasses through email\",\"A dentist extracting teeth over video call\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What happens to prescriptions and treatment plans after a telemedicine consultation?\",\"options\":[\"They are delayed until the patient visits the mainland\",\"They are issued immediately so patients can receive targeted treatment locally\",\"They are cancelled by the provincial governor\",\"They are stored on paper files for future years\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What major medical limitation of telemedicine is openly recognized in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It cannot conduct simple blood pressure checks\",\"It cannot replace emergency surgical operating facilities\",\"It is only functional during the daytime\",\"Doctors refuse to talk to nurses over video\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What economic benefit did rural families gain from the telemedicine network?\",\"options\":[\"They were exempt from paying any local taxes\",\"They reduced travel expenses and avoided costly mainland transfers\",\"They received cash allowances for every appointment\",\"They sold internet access to tourists\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What two infrastructure requirements are crucial to sustain the telemedicine program?\",\"options\":[\"More paved airport runways and commercial ports\",\"Expanding rural electrical grids and ensuring reliable satellite bandwidth\",\"Larger hospital parking lots and luxury waiting rooms\",\"Manufacturing diesel generators locally\"],\"correct\":1}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(15,'GRADE 10 - PRE-TEST - Historic Urban Conservation and Adaptive Reuse','Pre-Test','Instructional',10,'<p>Across expanding metropolitan centers in the Philippines, rapid modernization frequently places architectural heritage in direct conflict with real estate development. Centuries-old ancestral mansions, colonial civic plazas, and mid-century modern edifices are frequently demolished to make way for high-rise condominiums and commercial shopping complexes. Critics of preservation often argue that maintaining antiquated structures is economically impractical and impedes urban progress.</p><p>In response to this dilemma, progressive urban planners and architectural historians advocate for \'adaptive reuse.\' This conservation strategy involves repurposing historical buildings for contemporary functions while meticulously preserving their exterior facades, structural heritage, and distinct cultural character. Instead of abandoning historic structures to decay or condemning them to the wrecking ball, adaptive reuse revitalizes buildings as libraries, boutique cultural hotels, art galleries, educational centers, or civic offices.</p><p>Case studies from historic districts like the Heritage City of Vigan and Manila\'s Intramuros demonstrate the tangible economic benefits of adaptive reuse. Restored architectural corridors stimulate heritage tourism, generating sustainable employment for local artisans, restaurateurs, and hospitality providers. Furthermore, studies in urban ecology demonstrate that retrofitting existing brick and hardwood structures generates significantly less carbon emissions and demolition landfill waste than building entirely new concrete towers from scratch.</p><p>Ultimately, a city\'s historical buildings serve as irreplaceable tangible links to its cultural identity and collective memory. Preserving these historic monuments does not require freezing urban centers in the past; rather, thoughtful adaptive reuse demonstrates that historical authenticity and contemporary socioeconomic vitality can flourish together.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What primary urban conflict is identified in the opening paragraph?\",\"options\":[\"Competition between public bus and train transportation\",\"Architectural heritage preservation versus rapid real estate development\",\"Disputes over agricultural land boundaries\",\"Conflicts between coastal fishermen and shipping lines\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What common argument is raised by opponents of architectural conservation?\",\"options\":[\"Historical buildings are too sturdy to renovate\",\"Preserving antiquated structures is economically impractical and halts progress\",\"Old buildings consume less energy than modern ones\",\"Heritage structures attract too many foreign visitors\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the core definition of \'adaptive reuse\' in urban planning?\",\"options\":[\"Demolishing old buildings to create open green parks\",\"Repurposing historic buildings for contemporary functions while conserving their character\",\"Building exact plastic replicas of ancient monuments\",\"Relocating old buildings to rural provinces\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which of the following is an example of adaptive reuse mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Transforming a historic building into a library, gallery, or boutique cultural hotel\",\"Paving over a colonial plaza to build a multi-level parking garage\",\"Replacing wooden windows with aluminum louvers\",\"Selling ancestral land to foreign mining companies\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"Which two Philippine historic districts are highlighted as successful models?\",\"options\":[\"Bonifacio Global City and Eastwood\",\"Vigan and Intramuros\",\"Mactan and Boracay\",\"Subic Bay and Clark\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How does adaptive reuse stimulate economic prosperity in historic communities?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing real estate demolition fines\",\"By boosting heritage tourism and creating jobs for artisans and service providers\",\"By converting all residences into government tax offices\",\"By banning private vehicular traffic permanently\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What ecological advantage does retrofitting old structures offer over new construction?\",\"options\":[\"It eliminates the need for electric air conditioning\",\"It generates significantly less carbon emissions and landfill waste\",\"It increases the amount of concrete produced\",\"It cools down the entire surrounding city\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What do historic monuments represent for a society according to the author?\",\"options\":[\"Temporary obstacles to commercial real estate expansion\",\"Irreplaceable tangible links to cultural identity and collective memory\",\"Costly museum relics that have lost civic value\",\"Dangerous fire hazards that should be cleared\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What false choice does the author reject regarding historic preservation?\",\"options\":[\"That cities must choose between economic collapse and war\",\"That preservation requires freezing cities in the past rather than coexisting with vitality\",\"That historic preservation should be funded only by private donors\",\"That stone buildings cannot withstand tropical storms\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the overarching thesis of the passage?\",\"options\":[\"All old structures should eventually be replaced by steel skyscrapers\",\"Adaptive reuse proves that historical authenticity and modern economic vitality can thrive together\",\"Urban development should be prohibited inside all Philippine cities\",\"Tourism is the sole purpose of architectural conservation\"],\"correct\":1}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24'),(16,'GRADE 10 - POST-TEST - The Circular Economy and Sustainable Consumption','Post-Test','Instructional',10,'<p>Since the onset of the Industrial Revolution, global economic productivity has been driven almost exclusively by a linear consumption framework commonly described as the \'take-make-dispose\' model. Industrial enterprises extract finite virgin raw materials, manufacture consumer goods, and sell them to consumers who discard them after brief periods of utility. This linear paradigm has precipitated catastrophic ecological crises, including rampant plastic pollution, toxic industrial run-off, and the rapid depletion of critical mineral resources.</p><p>In response to these systemic vulnerabilities, progressive economists, industrial engineers, and environmental scientists advocate for a paradigm shift toward a \'circular economy.\' Unlike linear production, a circular economy is restorative and regenerative by design. It aims to decouple economic prosperity from finite resource consumption by designing waste out of the industrial lifecycle entirely. In a closed-loop system, products and materials are engineered for durability, modular repair, disassembly, and continuous remanufacturing.</p><p>Achieving a fully circular economy requires sweeping systemic innovations across international supply chains. Designers are adopting \'cradle-to-cradle\' principles, where materials are selected for non-toxic biodegradability or infinite recyclability. Simultaneously, business models are shifting from individual product ownership toward service-oriented subscriptions, where manufacturers retain legal ownership of goods and assume direct responsibility for upgrading, repairing, and recycling them at the end of their operational lifespans.</p><p>Transitioning away from centuries-old linear industrial practices requires robust legislative frameworks, corporate accountability, and informed consumer behavior. In developing nations like the Philippines, circular practices must also integrate the informal waste-picker sector into dignified, formal recycling cooperatives. Ultimately, embracing a circular economy is not merely an environmental obligation; it represents the only viable pathway toward sustainable long-term economic resilience.</p>','[{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How is the traditional linear economic paradigm summarized in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"The \'cradle-to-grave\' lifecycle\",\"The \'take-make-dispose\' model\",\"The \'extract-preserve-renew\' model\",\"The \'buy-borrow-return\' model\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What severe ecological problems have resulted from the linear consumption framework?\",\"options\":[\"Rampant plastic pollution, toxic runoff, and depletion of finite minerals\",\"Overabundance of forests and excess clean groundwater\",\"Decreasing global consumer demand and factory closures\",\"Too many products being repaired instead of sold\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the fundamental goal of a circular economy?\",\"options\":[\"To halt all global manufacturing and trade\",\"To decouple economic prosperity from finite resource consumption by eliminating waste\",\"To increase single-use packaging for hygiene\",\"To lower corporate taxes for mining companies\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How are consumer goods engineered within a closed-loop circular system?\",\"options\":[\"For planned obsolescence so they break quickly\",\"For durability, modular repair, disassembly, and continuous remanufacturing\",\"With cheap non-recyclable synthetic adhesives\",\"To be discarded directly into municipal incinerators\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What do \'cradle-to-cradle\' design principles require when selecting raw materials?\",\"options\":[\"Choosing toxic chemical coatings for fast assembly\",\"Selecting materials for non-toxic biodegradability or infinite recyclability\",\"Using virgin petroleum plastics exclusively\",\"Minimizing the lifespan of consumer appliances\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"How is consumer business shifting under the product-as-a-service model?\",\"options\":[\"Consumers must permanently buy every tool they use\",\"Manufacturers retain ownership and provide subscriptions while maintaining repair responsibility\",\"Companies refuse to accept returned equipment\",\"Products cannot be upgraded after purchase\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What responsibility do manufacturers hold under service-oriented subscription models?\",\"options\":[\"Dumping defective products into ocean landfills\",\"Directly upgrading, repairing, and recycling goods at the end of their operational life\",\"Charging customers for recycling permits\",\"Eliminating product warranties\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What specific recommendation does the author make for developing nations such as the Philippines?\",\"options\":[\"Banning all domestic recycling activities\",\"Integrating the informal waste-picker sector into dignified, formal recycling cooperatives\",\"Exporting all domestic municipal plastic to neighboring nations\",\"Closing down all municipal waste collection centers\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What three elements are cited as necessary to transition away from linear practices?\",\"options\":[\"Higher advertising budgets, larger malls, and cheaper plastics\",\"Robust legislative frameworks, corporate accountability, and informed consumer behavior\",\"Lower import tariffs, relaxed pollution laws, and fossil fuels\",\"Government subsidies for single-use packaging companies\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"What is the overarching conclusion of the passage regarding circular economics?\",\"options\":[\"It is an expensive luxury that developing countries cannot afford\",\"It is not merely an environmental duty, but the only viable path to long-term economic resilience\",\"It will be quickly replaced by more aggressive resource extraction models\",\"It works only for agricultural produce and not manufactured electronics\"],\"correct\":1}]','Published',NULL,'2026-10-11 10:41:24','2026-10-11 10:41:24');
