INSERT INTO `gst_assessments` VALUES (1,'Grade 7',40,'[{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p>Directions<span class=\\\"ff2 ws0\\\">: Read each selection and answer the questions that follow. Choose only the best answer.<br><br></span></p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h8 y15b ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><strong>What Little Things!</strong></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x6 h10 y15c ff2 fs7 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><em>By <span class=\\\"fc2\\\">Madison Julius Cawein </span></em></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4f h10 y15d ff2 fs7 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><em>From \\\"One Day and Another<br><br></em>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x50 h4 y15e ff2 fs7 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><span class=\\\"fs2\\\">What little things are those </span></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x51 h4 y15f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">That hold our happiness!</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x51 h4 y160 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">A smile, a glance, a rose</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x2d h4 y161 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Dropped from her hair or dress;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x16 h4 y162 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">A word, a look, a touch,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x52 h4 y163 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">These are so much, so much.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x53 h4 y164 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">An air we can\'t forget;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y165 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">A sunset\'s gold that gleams;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x3 h4 y166 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">A spray of mignonette,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y5e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Will fill the soul with dreams</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x50 h4 y167 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">More than all history says,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x54 h4 y168 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Or romance of old days.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x16 h4 y169 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">For of the human heart,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x53 h4 y16a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Not brain, is memory;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y16b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">These things it makes a part</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x56 h4 y16c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Of its own entity;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y16d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">The joys, the pains whereof</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x51 h4 y16e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Are the very food of love.</div>\\n</div>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y170 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Which of the following are \'little things\' that hold happiness?</div>\",\"options\":[\"air, perfume, hair\",\"flower, praise, look\",\"joy, food, smile\",\"word, touch, gold \"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In the 2nd stanza, what does the word, \'gleams\' mean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"beams\",\" dreams \",\"reflects\",\" shines\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What general truth is expressed in the 2nd stanza?</p>\",\"options\":[\" Air is a basic need of everyone. \",\"Everyone loves to dream. \",\"Love and romance are the same.\",\" The sunset\'s color is gold. \"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following contains a figure of speech?</p>\",\"options\":[\"A sunset\'s gold that gleams.\",\"More than all history says.\",\"Not brain, is memory.\",\"What little things are those.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What sensory imagery was mostly used in stanza 1?</p>\",\"options\":[\" Auditory/Sound\",\"Gustatory/Taste \",\"Olfactory/Smell \",\" Visual/Sight \"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following are themes expressed in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"contentment, joy, memory \",\"love, nature, happiness\",\"romance, pain, health\",\"sadness, joy, history \"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 ydc ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">What does the line,&nbsp;<span class=\\\"ff5\\\">&ldquo;The joys, the pains whereof, Are the very food of </span></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y18d ff5 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">love&rdquo; <span class=\\\"ff2\\\">mean? </span></div>\",\"options\":[\"One must experience sadness to experience love \",\"Sad and happy experiences make love grow\",\"To love is to accept imperfections and limitations\",\"Love is a beautiful experience and must be shared\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y192 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">What is the message of the poem?</div>\",\"options\":[\" Happiness can be found in little things. \",\"Little things are part of memories. \",\" The little things in life are important.\",\"To live is to appreciate little things.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the general tone of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Cheerful\",\"Funny \",\"Optimistic\",\"Serious\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the author&rsquo;s purpose in writing the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"To act it out on stage \",\"To express emotions\",\"To narrate a story\",\"To praise a loved one\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 1: The Solar System</strong></p><p>Our solar system consists of our star, the Sun, and everything bound to it by gravity. This includes the eight planets, dozens of moons, and millions of asteroids, comets, and meteoroids. The inner rocky planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars. The outer planets are gas giants Jupiter and Saturn, and ice giants Uranus and Neptune. Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a region populated by icy bodies like Pluto. The sun contains 99.8% of the mass in the solar system, making its gravitational pull immense enough to keep all these objects in orbit.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what holds the solar system together?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic fields\",\"Gravity\",\"Solar winds\",\"Dark matter\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following is considered an ice giant?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter\",\"Mars\",\"Neptune\",\"Venus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the solar system\'s mass is contained in the Sun?</p>\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"75.5%\",\"90%\",\"99.8%\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What lies beyond Neptune?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The Asteroid Belt\",\"The Kuiper Belt\",\"The Milky Way core\",\"The Andromeda Galaxy\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which planet is categorized as an inner rocky planet?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Saturn\",\"Jupiter\",\"Earth\",\"Uranus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The main purpose of this passage is to:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Persuade readers to study astronomy\",\"Describe the components of our solar system\",\"Explain how gravity works\",\"Compare Earth to Mars\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 2: The History of the Olympic Games</strong></p><p>The ancient Olympic Games were originally a festival, or celebration, of and for Zeus. They were held in Olympia, a rural sanctuary site in the western Peloponnesos. The Games were held every four years, and during this time, a truce was announced so athletes could travel safely. The first modern Olympics took place in 1896 in Athens, Greece, and featured 280 participants from 13 nations, competing in 43 events. Unlike the ancient games which only allowed freeborn Greek men to participate, the modern games have grown to include male and female athletes from all over the world.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who were the ancient Olympic Games meant to celebrate?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Apollo\",\"Zeus\",\"Athena\",\"Poseidon\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Where were the first modern Olympics held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Rome\",\"Paris\",\"Athens\",\"Olympia\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did the first modern Olympics take place?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1896\",\"1900\",\"1800\",\"1924\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How frequently were the ancient Games held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Every year\",\"Every two years\",\"Every four years\",\"Every decade\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was allowed to participate in the ancient Olympic games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Anyone from the world\",\"Only freeborn Greek men\",\"Men and women from Greece\",\"Professional athletes only\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why was a truce announced during the ancient Games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"To allow athletes safe travel\",\"To stop all wars permanently\",\"To honor the gods\",\"To allow farmers to harvest\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 3: The Benefits of Regular Exercise</strong></p><p>Regular physical activity is one of the most important things you can do for your health. Being physically active can improve your brain health, help manage weight, reduce the risk of disease, strengthen bones and muscles, and improve your ability to do everyday activities. Adults who sit less and do any amount of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity gain some health benefits. Furthermore, exercise promotes better sleep, boosting energy levels throughout the day. It also stimulates various brain chemicals that may leave you feeling happier, more relaxed, and less anxious.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what is one mental benefit of exercise?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It increases anxiety\",\"It stimulates brain chemicals that make you happier\",\"It makes you sleep less\",\"It decreases your ability to relax\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What effect does exercise have on everyday activities?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It makes them harder\",\"It has no effect\",\"It improves your ability to do them\",\"It makes them unnecessary\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does physical activity affect bones and muscles?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It weakens them\",\"It strengthens them\",\"It makes them heavier\",\"It causes them to shrink\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The passage suggests that adults should:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Sit more often\",\"Avoid vigorous activities\",\"Sit less and do physical activity\",\"Only do light stretching\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does exercise affect sleep, according to the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It causes insomnia\",\"It promotes better sleep\",\"It makes you sleep too much\",\"It has no connection to sleep\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the overall tone of this passage?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Informative and encouraging\",\"Negative and warning\",\"Humorous\",\"Argumentative\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 4: The Invention of the Telephone</strong></p><p>While Alexander Graham Bell is most often credited with the invention of the telephone, the true history is much more complex. Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant, began developing the design of a talking telegraph in 1849. Elisha Gray also designed a device that could transmit speech electrically. However, Bell was the first to secure the patent for the telephone in 1876. Bell\'s famous first words transmitted through the device were to his assistant: \\\"Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.\\\" This invention revolutionized global communication, making the world feel much smaller.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who is most often credited with inventing the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Elisha Gray\",\"Thomas Edison\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>&nbsp;In what year did Bell secure the patent for the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1849\",\"1876\",\"1896\",\"1900\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was the Italian immigrant that began developing a talking telegraph in 1849?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Leonardo da Vinci\",\"Guglielmo Marconi\",\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Enrico Fermi\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>&nbsp;Who did Bell speak to during the first transmission?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Elisha Gray\",\"Mr. Watson\",\"His wife\",\"Antonio Meucci\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What did the invention of the telephone do for the world?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It revolutionized global communication\",\"It made writing letters obsolete immediately\",\"It caused a war\",\"It slowed down business\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the meaning of \'revolutionary\' as implied in the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Violent\",\"Causing a dramatic change\",\"Spinning in circles\",\"Ineffective\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 5: The Ocean\'s Ecosystem</strong></p><p>The ocean is the largest ecosystem on Earth, covering more than 70% of the planet\'s surface. It is home to a vast diversity of life, from microscopic phytoplankton to the massive blue whale. Phytoplankton form the base of the marine food web, using sunlight to produce energy through photosynthesis. Small fish eat these plankton, and they in turn are eaten by larger predators like sharks and dolphins. Coral reefs, often called the \'rainforests of the sea,\' support a quarter of all marine species despite covering less than 1% of the ocean floor. Protecting these habitats is crucial for marine biodiversity.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What percentage of the Earth\'s surface is covered by the ocean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"30%\",\"50%\",\"More than 70%\",\"90%\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What forms the base of the marine food web?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Coral reefs\",\"Sharks\",\"Blue whales\",\"Phytoplankton\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How do phytoplankton produce energy?</p>\",\"options\":[\"By eating small fish\",\"Through photosynthesis\",\"By consuming coral\",\"By swimming constantly\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What are coral reefs often compared to?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The desert\",\"The rainforests of the sea\",\"The deep abyss\",\"Mountains\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Less than 1%\",\"10%\",\"25%\",\"50%\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why is protecting coral reefs important?</p>\",\"options\":[\"They provide drinking water\",\"They support a quarter of all marine species\",\"They prevent earthquakes\",\"They are a good source of oil\"],\"correct\":1}]'),(2,'Grade 8',40,'[{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p>Directions<span class=\\\"ff2 ws0\\\">: Read each selection and answer the questions that follow. Choose only the best answer.<br></span><br><br></p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x5e h8 y26b ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><strong>AFRICA\'S PLEA</strong></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x16 h13 y72 ff5 fs9 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><em>By Roland Tombekai Dempster<br><br></em>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y26c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I am not you</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y26d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">but you will not<br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y19 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">give me chance,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y1df ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">will not let me be <span class=\\\"ff5 ws2\\\">me</span>.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y26e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">&ldquo;If I were you&rdquo;-</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y26f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">but you know</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y13a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I am not you,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y270 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">yet you will not</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y271 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">let me be <span class=\\\"ff5 ws2\\\">me</span>.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y272 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You meddle, interfere</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y273 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">in my affairs</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y274 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">as if they were yours</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y275 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">and you were me.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y276 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You are unfair, unwise,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y277 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">foolish to think</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y278 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">that I can be you,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y279 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">talk, act</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y27a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">and think like you.<br><br></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y27b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">God made me <span class=\\\"ff5 ws2\\\">me</span>.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y27c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">He made you <span class=\\\"ff5 ws2\\\">you</span>.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y27d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">For God\'s sake</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4e h4 y27e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Let me be me.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y27f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">&nbsp;</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who is speaking in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"child\",\"country \",\" man\",\" people\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which has the same meaning as the word meddle in the third stanza?</p>\",\"options\":[\"not accepted\",\"not allowed \",\"not friendly\",\"not recognized\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following are the meanings of the word&nbsp;<span class=\\\"ff5\\\">plea </span>as expressed in the poem?<br><br></p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x8 h4 y15 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I. a call to change values</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x8 h4 y28a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">II. a demand for acceptance</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x8 h4 y28b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">III. a request for appreciation</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x8 h4 y28c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">IV. a request to be treated equality<br>V. an appeal for unity</div>\",\"options\":[\"I, II, and III\",\"I, IV, and V \",\" II, III, and IV\",\"III, IV, and V\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which social issue is NOT tackled in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"discrimination\",\"equality \",\"independence\",\"poverty \"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In which stanza is the uniqueness of each individual implied?</p>\",\"options\":[\"stanza 2\",\"stanza 3 \",\"stanza 4\",\"stanza 5\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the general tone of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"angry\",\"fearful\",\"gloomy \",\" hurt \"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following sentences does NOT state the theme of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"We are all unique human beings.\",\"We are equal in the eyes of God.\",\"We are the master of our lives.\",\"We can choose how to live our lives.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What did the author use in expressing the theme of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"dialogues\",\" imagery\",\"repetitions\",\"symbolisms\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>To whom is the writer directing this message?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Those who criticize people that they dislike. \",\"Those who destroy the reputation of others\",\"Those who discriminate colored people. \",\"Those who want to control other people.\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What type of poem is this poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Dramatic poem because it can be acted.\",\"Elegiac poem because it expresses sadness.\",\"Lyric poem because it expresses feelings.\",\"Narrative poem because it tells a story.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 1: The Solar System</strong></p><p>Our solar system consists of our star, the Sun, and everything bound to it by gravity. This includes the eight planets, dozens of moons, and millions of asteroids, comets, and meteoroids. The inner rocky planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars. The outer planets are gas giants Jupiter and Saturn, and ice giants Uranus and Neptune. Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a region populated by icy bodies like Pluto. The sun contains 99.8% of the mass in the solar system, making its gravitational pull immense enough to keep all these objects in orbit.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what holds the solar system together?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic fields\",\"Gravity\",\"Solar winds\",\"Dark matter\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following is considered an ice giant?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter\",\"Mars\",\"Neptune\",\"Venus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the solar system\'s mass is contained in the Sun?</p>\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"75.5%\",\"90%\",\"99.8%\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What lies beyond Neptune?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The Asteroid Belt\",\"The Kuiper Belt\",\"The Milky Way core\",\"The Andromeda Galaxy\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which planet is categorized as an inner rocky planet?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Saturn\",\"Jupiter\",\"Earth\",\"Uranus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The main purpose of this passage is to:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Persuade readers to study astronomy\",\"Describe the components of our solar system\",\"Explain how gravity works\",\"Compare Earth to Mars\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 2: The History of the Olympic Games</strong></p><p>The ancient Olympic Games were originally a festival, or celebration, of and for Zeus. They were held in Olympia, a rural sanctuary site in the western Peloponnesos. The Games were held every four years, and during this time, a truce was announced so athletes could travel safely. The first modern Olympics took place in 1896 in Athens, Greece, and featured 280 participants from 13 nations, competing in 43 events. Unlike the ancient games which only allowed freeborn Greek men to participate, the modern games have grown to include male and female athletes from all over the world.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who were the ancient Olympic Games meant to celebrate?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Apollo\",\"Zeus\",\"Athena\",\"Poseidon\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Where were the first modern Olympics held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Rome\",\"Paris\",\"Athens\",\"Olympia\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did the first modern Olympics take place?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1896\",\"1900\",\"1800\",\"1924\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How frequently were the ancient Games held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Every year\",\"Every two years\",\"Every four years\",\"Every decade\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was allowed to participate in the ancient Olympic games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Anyone from the world\",\"Only freeborn Greek men\",\"Men and women from Greece\",\"Professional athletes only\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why was a truce announced during the ancient Games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"To allow athletes safe travel\",\"To stop all wars permanently\",\"To honor the gods\",\"To allow farmers to harvest\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 3: The Benefits of Regular Exercise</strong></p><p>Regular physical activity is one of the most important things you can do for your health. Being physically active can improve your brain health, help manage weight, reduce the risk of disease, strengthen bones and muscles, and improve your ability to do everyday activities. Adults who sit less and do any amount of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity gain some health benefits. Furthermore, exercise promotes better sleep, boosting energy levels throughout the day. It also stimulates various brain chemicals that may leave you feeling happier, more relaxed, and less anxious.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what is one mental benefit of exercise?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It increases anxiety\",\"It stimulates brain chemicals that make you happier\",\"It makes you sleep less\",\"It decreases your ability to relax\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What effect does exercise have on everyday activities?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It makes them harder\",\"It has no effect\",\"It improves your ability to do them\",\"It makes them unnecessary\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does physical activity affect bones and muscles?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It weakens them\",\"It strengthens them\",\"It makes them heavier\",\"It causes them to shrink\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The passage suggests that adults should:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Sit more often\",\"Avoid vigorous activities\",\"Sit less and do physical activity\",\"Only do light stretching\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>&nbsp;How does exercise affect sleep, according to the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It causes insomnia\",\"It promotes better sleep\",\"It makes you sleep too much\",\"It has no connection to sleep\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the overall tone of this passage?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Informative and encouraging\",\"Negative and warning\",\"Humorous\",\"Argumentative\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 4: The Invention of the Telephone</strong></p><p>While Alexander Graham Bell is most often credited with the invention of the telephone, the true history is much more complex. Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant, began developing the design of a talking telegraph in 1849. Elisha Gray also designed a device that could transmit speech electrically. However, Bell was the first to secure the patent for the telephone in 1876. Bell\'s famous first words transmitted through the device were to his assistant: \\\"Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.\\\" This invention revolutionized global communication, making the world feel much smaller.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who is most often credited with inventing the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Elisha Gray\",\"Thomas Edison\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did Bell secure the patent for the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1849\",\"1876\",\"1896\",\"1900\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was the Italian immigrant that began developing a talking telegraph in 1849?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Leonardo da Vinci\",\"Guglielmo Marconi\",\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Enrico Fermi\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who did Bell speak to during the first transmission?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Elisha Gray\",\"Mr. Watson\",\"His wife\",\"Antonio Meucci\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What did the invention of the telephone do for the world?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It revolutionized global communication\",\"It made writing letters obsolete immediately\",\"It caused a war\",\"It slowed down business\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the meaning of \'revolutionary\' as implied in the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Violent\",\"Causing a dramatic change\",\"Spinning in circles\",\"Ineffective\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 5: The Ocean\'s Ecosystem</strong></p><p>The ocean is the largest ecosystem on Earth, covering more than 70% of the planet\'s surface. It is home to a vast diversity of life, from microscopic phytoplankton to the massive blue whale. Phytoplankton form the base of the marine food web, using sunlight to produce energy through photosynthesis. Small fish eat these plankton, and they in turn are eaten by larger predators like sharks and dolphins. Coral reefs, often called the \'rainforests of the sea,\' support a quarter of all marine species despite covering less than 1% of the ocean floor. Protecting these habitats is crucial for marine biodiversity.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What percentage of the Earth\'s surface is covered by the ocean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"30%\",\"50%\",\"More than 70%\",\"90%\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What forms the base of the marine food web?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Coral reefs\",\"Sharks\",\"Blue whales\",\"Phytoplankton\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How do phytoplankton produce energy?</p>\",\"options\":[\"By eating small fish\",\"Through photosynthesis\",\"By consuming coral\",\"By swimming constantly\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What are coral reefs often compared to?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The desert\",\"The rainforests of the sea\",\"The deep abyss\",\"Mountains\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Less than 1%\",\"10%\",\"25%\",\"50%\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why is protecting coral reefs important?</p>\",\"options\":[\"They provide drinking water\",\"They support a quarter of all marine species\",\"They prevent earthquakes\",\"They are a good source of oil\"],\"correct\":1}]'),(3,'Grade 9',40,'[{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p>Directions<span class=\\\"ff2 ws0\\\">: Read each selection and answer the questions that follow. Choose only the best answer.<br><br></span></p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x4f h8 y199 ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><strong>The Road Not Taken</strong></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h8 y350 ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls2 ws0\\\"><em><span class=\\\"ff5 fs3 ls0\\\">By Robert Frost<br><br></span></em></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h4 y351 ff5 fs3 fc0 sc0 ls3 ws0\\\"><span class=\\\"ff2 fs2 ls0\\\">Two roads diverged in a yellow wood, </span></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h4 y352 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And sorry I could not travel both</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h4 y353 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And be one traveler, long I stood</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h4 y354 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And looked down one as far as I could<br>To where it bent in the undergrowth;<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y219 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Then took the other, as just as fair,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y26e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And having perhaps the better claim,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y26f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Because it was grassy and wanted wear;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y13a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Though as for that the passing there</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y270 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Had worn them really about the same,<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y2c6 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And both that morning equally lay</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y272 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">In leaves no step had trodden black.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y273 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Oh, I kept the first for another day!</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y274 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Yet knowing how way leads on to way,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y275 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I doubted if I should ever come back.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y276 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y276 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I shall be telling this with a sigh</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y277 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Somewhere ages and ages hence:</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y278 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Two roads diverged in a wood, and I&mdash;</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x69 h4 y279 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I took the one less traveled by,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x34 h4 y27a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">And that has made all the difference.<br><br></div>\\n<br><br></div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which road was chosen by the persona in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The persona chose both roads.\",\"The persona chose the less used road. \",\"The persona chose the more used road \",\"The persona chose none of them.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is suggested by the \\\"yellow wood\\\" in the first line?</p>\",\"options\":[\"color of the wood \",\"place in the forest\",\"season of the year\",\"time of the day\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which word in the poem is the same in meaning as &lsquo;has stepped on&rsquo;?</p>\",\"options\":[\"diverged\",\"travelled \",\" trodden \",\"wanted\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which line contains a figure of speech?</p>\",\"options\":[\"And both that morning equally lay \",\"Somewhere ages and ages hence\",\"Then took the other, as just as fair\",\"Yet knowing how way leads on to way \"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of these is implied in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The persona is on his/her way back home.\",\"The persona is travelling far from home.\",\"The persona is with other travellers.\",\"The persona has been travelling for days.\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y36a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">What was the dilemma of the persona in the first stanza of the</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y36b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">poem?</div>\",\"options\":[\"He can only choose one road.\",\"He feels lonely traveling alone.\",\"He lacks courage to travel.\",\"He regrets the road he chose. \"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p><span class=\\\"ff2 fs2 ls0 ws0\\\">What mood is created in the poem?</span></p>\",\"options\":[\"Cheerful- happy\",\"Creepy- frightening\",\" Idyllic- peaceful \",\"Grateful- thankful\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What do the two roads stand for?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Achievements in life \",\"Choices in life \",\"Happiness in life\",\"Priorities in life\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y377 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Which of the following techniques did the author use to express the theme of the</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y378 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">poem?</div>\",\"options\":[\"Use of imagery\",\"Use of literary devices\",\"Use of rhymes\",\"Use of symbolisms\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which is NOT a theme of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Accepting challenges\",\"Being unconventional\",\"Knowing one’s priorities\",\"Making the right choices\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 1: The Solar System</strong></p><p>Our solar system consists of our star, the Sun, and everything bound to it by gravity. This includes the eight planets, dozens of moons, and millions of asteroids, comets, and meteoroids. The inner rocky planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars. The outer planets are gas giants Jupiter and Saturn, and ice giants Uranus and Neptune. Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a region populated by icy bodies like Pluto. The sun contains 99.8% of the mass in the solar system, making its gravitational pull immense enough to keep all these objects in orbit.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what holds the solar system together?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic fields\",\"Gravity\",\"Solar winds\",\"Dark matter\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following is considered an ice giant?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter\",\"Mars\",\"Neptune\",\"Venus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the solar system\'s mass is contained in the Sun?</p>\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"75.5%\",\"90%\",\"99.8%\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What lies beyond Neptune?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The Asteroid Belt\",\"The Kuiper Belt\",\"The Milky Way core\",\"The Andromeda Galaxy\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which planet is categorized as an inner rocky planet?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Saturn\",\"Jupiter\",\"Earth\",\"Uranus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The main purpose of this passage is to:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Persuade readers to study astronomy\",\"Describe the components of our solar system\",\"Explain how gravity works\",\"Compare Earth to Mars\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 2: The History of the Olympic Games</strong></p><p>The ancient Olympic Games were originally a festival, or celebration, of and for Zeus. They were held in Olympia, a rural sanctuary site in the western Peloponnesos. The Games were held every four years, and during this time, a truce was announced so athletes could travel safely. The first modern Olympics took place in 1896 in Athens, Greece, and featured 280 participants from 13 nations, competing in 43 events. Unlike the ancient games which only allowed freeborn Greek men to participate, the modern games have grown to include male and female athletes from all over the world.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who were the ancient Olympic Games meant to celebrate?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Apollo\",\"Zeus\",\"Athena\",\"Poseidon\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Where were the first modern Olympics held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Rome\",\"Paris\",\"Athens\",\"Olympia\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did the first modern Olympics take place?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1896\",\"1900\",\"1800\",\"1924\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How frequently were the ancient Games held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Every year\",\"Every two years\",\"Every four years\",\"Every decade\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was allowed to participate in the ancient Olympic games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Anyone from the world\",\"Only freeborn Greek men\",\"Men and women from Greece\",\"Professional athletes only\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why was a truce announced during the ancient Games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"To allow athletes safe travel\",\"To stop all wars permanently\",\"To honor the gods\",\"To allow farmers to harvest\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 3: The Benefits of Regular Exercise</strong></p><p>Regular physical activity is one of the most important things you can do for your health. Being physically active can improve your brain health, help manage weight, reduce the risk of disease, strengthen bones and muscles, and improve your ability to do everyday activities. Adults who sit less and do any amount of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity gain some health benefits. Furthermore, exercise promotes better sleep, boosting energy levels throughout the day. It also stimulates various brain chemicals that may leave you feeling happier, more relaxed, and less anxious.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what is one mental benefit of exercise?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It increases anxiety\",\"It stimulates brain chemicals that make you happier\",\"It makes you sleep less\",\"It decreases your ability to relax\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What effect does exercise have on everyday activities?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It makes them harder\",\"It has no effect\",\"It improves your ability to do them\",\"It makes them unnecessary\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does physical activity affect bones and muscles?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It weakens them\",\"It strengthens them\",\"It makes them heavier\",\"It causes them to shrink\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The passage suggests that adults should:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Sit more often\",\"Avoid vigorous activities\",\"Sit less and do physical activity\",\"Only do light stretching\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does exercise affect sleep, according to the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It causes insomnia\",\"It promotes better sleep\",\"It makes you sleep too much\",\"It has no connection to sleep\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the overall tone of this passage?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Informative and encouraging\",\"Negative and warning\",\"Humorous\",\"Argumentative\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 4: The Invention of the Telephone</strong></p><p>While Alexander Graham Bell is most often credited with the invention of the telephone, the true history is much more complex. Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant, began developing the design of a talking telegraph in 1849. Elisha Gray also designed a device that could transmit speech electrically. However, Bell was the first to secure the patent for the telephone in 1876. Bell\'s famous first words transmitted through the device were to his assistant: \\\"Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.\\\" This invention revolutionized global communication, making the world feel much smaller.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who is most often credited with inventing the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Elisha Gray\",\"Thomas Edison\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did Bell secure the patent for the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1849\",\"1876\",\"1896\",\"1900\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was the Italian immigrant that began developing a talking telegraph in 1849?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Leonardo da Vinci\",\"Guglielmo Marconi\",\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Enrico Fermi\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who did Bell speak to during the first transmission?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Elisha Gray\",\"Mr. Watson\",\"His wife\",\"Antonio Meucci\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What did the invention of the telephone do for the world?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It revolutionized global communication\",\"It made writing letters obsolete immediately\",\"It caused a war\",\"It slowed down business\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the meaning of \'revolutionary\' as implied in the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Violent\",\"Causing a dramatic change\",\"Spinning in circles\",\"Ineffective\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 5: The Ocean\'s Ecosystem</strong></p><p>The ocean is the largest ecosystem on Earth, covering more than 70% of the planet\'s surface. It is home to a vast diversity of life, from microscopic phytoplankton to the massive blue whale. Phytoplankton form the base of the marine food web, using sunlight to produce energy through photosynthesis. Small fish eat these plankton, and they in turn are eaten by larger predators like sharks and dolphins. Coral reefs, often called the \'rainforests of the sea,\' support a quarter of all marine species despite covering less than 1% of the ocean floor. Protecting these habitats is crucial for marine biodiversity.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What percentage of the Earth\'s surface is covered by the ocean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"30%\",\"50%\",\"More than 70%\",\"90%\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What forms the base of the marine food web?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Coral reefs\",\"Sharks\",\"Blue whales\",\"Phytoplankton\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How do phytoplankton produce energy?</p>\",\"options\":[\"By eating small fish\",\"Through photosynthesis\",\"By consuming coral\",\"By swimming constantly\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What are coral reefs often compared to?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The desert\",\"The rainforests of the sea\",\"The deep abyss\",\"Mountains\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Less than 1%\",\"10%\",\"25%\",\"50%\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why is protecting coral reefs important?</p>\",\"options\":[\"They provide drinking water\",\"They support a quarter of all marine species\",\"They prevent earthquakes\",\"They are a good source of oil\"],\"correct\":1}]'),(4,'Grade 10',40,'[{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p>Directions<span class=\\\"ff2 ws0\\\">: Read each selection and answer the questions that follow. Choose only the best answer.<br><br></span></p>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h8 y1a1 ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\"><strong>Still I Rise</strong></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h8 y43e ff1 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls2 ws0\\\"><em><span class=\\\"ff5 fs9 ls0\\\">By Maya Angelou<br><br><br></span></em>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y43f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You may write me down in history</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y440 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">With your bitter, twisted lies,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y441 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You may trod me in the very dirt</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y442 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">But still, like dust, I\'ll rise.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y443 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Does my sassiness upset you?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 yd7 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Why are you beset with gloom?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y444 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">&rsquo;Cause I walk like I\'ve got oil wells</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y445 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Pumping in my living room.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y446 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Just like moons and like suns,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y447 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">With the certainty of tides,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y448 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Just like hopes springing high,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y449 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Still I\'ll rise.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y44a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Did you want to see me broken?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y2b7 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Bowed head and lowered eyes?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y44b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Shoulders falling like teardrops,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y44c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Weakened by my soulful cries?<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y44d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Does my haughtiness offend you?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y44e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Don\'t you take it awful hard?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y44f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">&rsquo;Cause I laugh like I\'ve got gold mines</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y450 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Diggin&rsquo; in my own backyard.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y451 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You may shoot me with your words,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y452 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You may cut me with your eyes,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y453 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">You may kill me with your hatefulness,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y454 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">But still, like air, I&rsquo;ll rise.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y3df ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Does my sexiness upset you?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y455 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Does it come as a surprise?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y456 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">That I dance like I\'ve got diamonds<br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y135 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">At the meeting of my thighs?</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y457 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Out of the huts of history&rsquo;s shame</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y458 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y459 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Up from a past that&rsquo;s rooted in pain</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y1c7 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y45a ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I\'m a black ocean, leaping and wide,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y45b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Welling and swelling I bear in the tide.<br><br>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y45c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Leaving behind nights of terror and fear</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y45d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y15e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Into a daybreak that&rsquo;s wondrously clear</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y45e ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y45f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">Bringing the gifts that my ancestors gave,</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y460 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I am the dream and the hope of the slave.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y3d ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y461 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x48 h4 y462 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">I rise.</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n<em><span class=\\\"ff5 fs9 ls0\\\">&nbsp;</span></em></div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x55 h4 y43f ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">&nbsp;</div>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What upsets the one addressed by the persona in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"the persona’s arrogance\",\"the persona’s boldness\",\"the persona’s rudeness\",\"the persona’s weakness\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What does&nbsp;<span class=\\\"ff5\\\">trod </span>in the first stanza mean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"bend over\",\"blow over\",\"step over\",\"take over \"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following is implied about the persona in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The persona is a dancer.\",\"The persona is a slave.\",\"The persona is black\",\"The persona is rich.\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following lines contain a figure of speech?</p>\",\"options\":[\"But still, like dust, I\'ll rise.\",\"Because I walk like I\'ve got oil wells\",\"Does my haughtiness offend you?\",\"Leaving behind nights of terror and fear \"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the general tone of the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"defiant \",\"doubtful\",\"happy \",\"optimistic\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the rhyming pattern evident in the first stanza?</p>\",\"options\":[\"AABC\",\"ABBC \",\"ABCB\",\"ABCA\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y476 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">To what sense do the lines &ldquo;Into a daybreak that&rsquo;s wondrously clear, I rise&rdquo; appeal</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y477 ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">to?</div>\",\"options\":[\"sense of hearing\",\"sense of sight\",\"sense of smell\",\"sense of touch\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y47b ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">What relationship is evident between the persona speaking in the poem and the</div>\\n<div class=\\\"t m0 x18 h4 y47c ff2 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0\\\">person she is talking with?</div>\",\"options\":[\"a bully to a bullied\",\"a defender to an ally\",\"a friend to a friend \",\"a leader to a follower\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who are being addressed in the poem?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Those who experienced being abused.\",\"Those who felt extremely depressed.\",\"Those who lost interest in their lives.\",\"Those who were not appreciated.\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following techniques did the author use in emphasizing the theme?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Figures of speech such as metaphors are used. \",\"Rhyming patterns were used in every stanza.\",\"Significant lines were repeated all throughout the poem.\",\"The events are narrated using the third person point of view\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 1: The Solar System</strong></p><p>Our solar system consists of our star, the Sun, and everything bound to it by gravity. This includes the eight planets, dozens of moons, and millions of asteroids, comets, and meteoroids. The inner rocky planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars. The outer planets are gas giants Jupiter and Saturn, and ice giants Uranus and Neptune. Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a region populated by icy bodies like Pluto. The sun contains 99.8% of the mass in the solar system, making its gravitational pull immense enough to keep all these objects in orbit.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what holds the solar system together?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic fields\",\"Gravity\",\"Solar winds\",\"Dark matter\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which of the following is considered an ice giant?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter\",\"Mars\",\"Neptune\",\"Venus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the solar system\'s mass is contained in the Sun?</p>\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"75.5%\",\"90%\",\"99.8%\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What lies beyond Neptune?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The Asteroid Belt\",\"The Kuiper Belt\",\"The Milky Way core\",\"The Andromeda Galaxy\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Which planet is categorized as an inner rocky planet?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Saturn\",\"Jupiter\",\"Earth\",\"Uranus\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The main purpose of this passage is to:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Persuade readers to study astronomy\",\"Describe the components of our solar system\",\"Explain how gravity works\",\"Compare Earth to Mars\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 2: The History of the Olympic Games</strong></p><p>The ancient Olympic Games were originally a festival, or celebration, of and for Zeus. They were held in Olympia, a rural sanctuary site in the western Peloponnesos. The Games were held every four years, and during this time, a truce was announced so athletes could travel safely. The first modern Olympics took place in 1896 in Athens, Greece, and featured 280 participants from 13 nations, competing in 43 events. Unlike the ancient games which only allowed freeborn Greek men to participate, the modern games have grown to include male and female athletes from all over the world.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who were the ancient Olympic Games meant to celebrate?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Apollo\",\"Zeus\",\"Athena\",\"Poseidon\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Where were the first modern Olympics held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Rome\",\"Paris\",\"Athens\",\"Olympia\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did the first modern Olympics take place?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1896\",\"1900\",\"1800\",\"1924\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How frequently were the ancient Games held?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Every year\",\"Every two years\",\"Every four years\",\"Every decade\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was allowed to participate in the ancient Olympic games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Anyone from the world\",\"Only freeborn Greek men\",\"Men and women from Greece\",\"Professional athletes only\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why was a truce announced during the ancient Games?</p>\",\"options\":[\"To allow athletes safe travel\",\"To stop all wars permanently\",\"To honor the gods\",\"To allow farmers to harvest\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 3: The Benefits of Regular Exercise</strong></p><p>Regular physical activity is one of the most important things you can do for your health. Being physically active can improve your brain health, help manage weight, reduce the risk of disease, strengthen bones and muscles, and improve your ability to do everyday activities. Adults who sit less and do any amount of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity gain some health benefits. Furthermore, exercise promotes better sleep, boosting energy levels throughout the day. It also stimulates various brain chemicals that may leave you feeling happier, more relaxed, and less anxious.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>According to the passage, what is one mental benefit of exercise?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It increases anxiety\",\"It stimulates brain chemicals that make you happier\",\"It makes you sleep less\",\"It decreases your ability to relax\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What effect does exercise have on everyday activities?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It makes them harder\",\"It has no effect\",\"It improves your ability to do them\",\"It makes them unnecessary\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does physical activity affect bones and muscles?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It weakens them\",\"It strengthens them\",\"It makes them heavier\",\"It causes them to shrink\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>The passage suggests that adults should:</p>\",\"options\":[\"Sit more often\",\"Avoid vigorous activities\",\"Sit less and do physical activity\",\"Only do light stretching\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How does exercise affect sleep, according to the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It causes insomnia\",\"It promotes better sleep\",\"It makes you sleep too much\",\"It has no connection to sleep\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the overall tone of this passage?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Informative and encouraging\",\"Negative and warning\",\"Humorous\",\"Argumentative\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 4: The Invention of the Telephone</strong></p><p>While Alexander Graham Bell is most often credited with the invention of the telephone, the true history is much more complex. Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant, began developing the design of a talking telegraph in 1849. Elisha Gray also designed a device that could transmit speech electrically. However, Bell was the first to secure the patent for the telephone in 1876. Bell\'s famous first words transmitted through the device were to his assistant: \\\"Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.\\\" This invention revolutionized global communication, making the world feel much smaller.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who is most often credited with inventing the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Elisha Gray\",\"Thomas Edison\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>In what year did Bell secure the patent for the telephone?</p>\",\"options\":[\"1849\",\"1876\",\"1896\",\"1900\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who was the Italian immigrant that began developing a talking telegraph in 1849?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Leonardo da Vinci\",\"Guglielmo Marconi\",\"Antonio Meucci\",\"Enrico Fermi\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Who did Bell speak to during the first transmission?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Elisha Gray\",\"Mr. Watson\",\"His wife\",\"Antonio Meucci\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What did the invention of the telephone do for the world?</p>\",\"options\":[\"It revolutionized global communication\",\"It made writing letters obsolete immediately\",\"It caused a war\",\"It slowed down business\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What is the meaning of \'revolutionary\' as implied in the text?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Violent\",\"Causing a dramatic change\",\"Spinning in circles\",\"Ineffective\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"description\",\"question\":\"<p><strong>Reading Passage 5: The Ocean\'s Ecosystem</strong></p><p>The ocean is the largest ecosystem on Earth, covering more than 70% of the planet\'s surface. It is home to a vast diversity of life, from microscopic phytoplankton to the massive blue whale. Phytoplankton form the base of the marine food web, using sunlight to produce energy through photosynthesis. Small fish eat these plankton, and they in turn are eaten by larger predators like sharks and dolphins. Coral reefs, often called the \'rainforests of the sea,\' support a quarter of all marine species despite covering less than 1% of the ocean floor. Protecting these habitats is crucial for marine biodiversity.</p>\"},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What percentage of the Earth\'s surface is covered by the ocean?</p>\",\"options\":[\"30%\",\"50%\",\"More than 70%\",\"90%\"],\"correct\":2},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What forms the base of the marine food web?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Coral reefs\",\"Sharks\",\"Blue whales\",\"Phytoplankton\"],\"correct\":3},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How do phytoplankton produce energy?</p>\",\"options\":[\"By eating small fish\",\"Through photosynthesis\",\"By consuming coral\",\"By swimming constantly\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>What are coral reefs often compared to?</p>\",\"options\":[\"The desert\",\"The rainforests of the sea\",\"The deep abyss\",\"Mountains\"],\"correct\":1},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>How much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover?</p>\",\"options\":[\"Less than 1%\",\"10%\",\"25%\",\"50%\"],\"correct\":0},{\"type\":\"multichoice\",\"question\":\"<p>Why is protecting coral reefs important?</p>\",\"options\":[\"They provide drinking water\",\"They support a quarter of all marine species\",\"They prevent earthquakes\",\"They are a good source of oil\"],\"correct\":1}]');
